  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provision Of Life-Saving Multi-Sectoral Assistance To Internally Displaced Persons and Host Communities in Yemen</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">After more than eight years of conflict, the humanitarian needs continued to worsen across Yemen, especially for families living in protracted displacement and close to frontlines. There are 2,302,346 internally displaced persons (IDPs) and 1,240,944 IDP returnees in 13 governorates in the south of Yemen. In 2022, conflict-related reasons were still reported as a main driver of displacement and the needs of IDPs were aggravated by a weakened economy, rains and flooding, and fuel shortages. Vulnerable communities are likely to resort to negative coping mechanisms. Urgent support is needed, through an integrated evidence-based approach, for vulnerable households to purchase critical items and services in line with their needs; have access to safe water, health and sanitation services; and receive specialized and general protection and camp coordination and camp management services. 
 
In line with an ongoing Bureau for Humanitarian Assistance funded project, as well as Yemen&#x2019;s 2023 Humanitarian Response Plan and the International Organization for Migration (IOM)&#x2019;s 2023 Crisis Response Plan, the Organization is contributing towards alleviating immediate suffering of food insecure populations with timely and high impact multi-sectoral interventions, as well as complementary interventions, such as assessments through the DTM, that are crucial for informing the integrated response.</narrative>
  </description>
